From 8807b49b3ff09bad096424c3e0c8aa9128704ff1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 07:53:28 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08882/11362] exp6256: score the goal predicate two-sided; a
 stored predicate is already degenerate

Follow-up to exp6255, which found the win exemplar HURTS dynamics fidelity but
scored the ENGINE only. The exemplar's purpose is arguably the GOAL predicate, so
that negative may be measuring the wrong half. exp6256 scores the half the
exemplar is aimed at.

Scored two-sided on purpose. Held-out transitions contain no level-ups, so
specificity alone is a false-positive check that a return-False-everywhere
predicate passes perfectly. Sensitivity -- does it fire on a grid where a level-up
really happened, supplied by replay_win_transition -- is what makes either number
mean anything.

That design paid off before the run started. A smoke test against dc22's engine
already in the tracked store: goal_specificity_accuracy 1.0, zero false
positives, and goal_fires_on_real_win FALSE. A perfect score on a predicate that
never fires on the actual win. Since collect_transitions yields no level-ups this
is structurally one-sided for every game, so any pipeline gating on that accuracy
alone will accept constant-False predicates. arc_llm_reinduction already carries
degenerate_goal_predicate rejection, so the project knows the failure mode exists;
what is new is that the CONSISTENCY score gives a degenerate predicate a perfect
mark, so the two mechanisms are not interchangeable. Recorded in
ops/known-issues.md; a CPU-only sweep of all 25 stored engines would say how
widespread it is.

This run also SAVES the induced sources in the artifact. exp6255 discarded them,
which is the only reason answering this question required re-inducing on GPU.

